# Example Suricata threshold file for a NetWatch sensor. Copy it to the sensor's Suricata
# threshold.config and replace the example addresses (RFC 5737 / RFC 3849 documentation ranges)
# with your own. tests/test_suricata_tuning.py enforces the rule below on this file.
#
# Rule: every suppression is narrow. Either it names exactly one signature plus one exact source
# or destination, or it is a pure service-identification signature (one named benign service,
# nothing else) suppressed sensor-wide. Suricata still writes and retains the dns/tls/http/flow
# records for suppressed traffic; only the alert label is dropped.

# STUN binding request/response: WebRTC and VPN NAT traversal, informational noise
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2016149
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2016150

# ET DOS SSDP amplification scan: a VPN client's port mapper sends unicast M-SEARCH to the LAN
# gateway; only the gateway as destination is exempt
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2019102, track by_dst, ip 192.0.2.1

# ET SCAN Potential SSH Scan OUTBOUND: git over SSH to GitHub's published range
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2003068, track by_dst, ip 140.82.112.0/20

# Go-http-client UA from one known host's VPN health probes, that host only
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2060251, track by_src, ip 192.0.2.20

# Plain-HTTP content fetches from one CDN block (IPv6 documentation prefix as the example)
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2062715, track by_dst, ip 2001:db8:7400::/48
